finding: Corrections required: the gate proves a weaker claim than zero sampled bytes outside the one submission's encoder/pass encoders/command buffer. 1. Highest cost: the supposed exact call-site exceptions are whole-function attribution buckets, and demonstrably swallow engine JavaScript. Independent baseline sample on HEAD qualified all three 600-frame windows (warm 6000/12000/A-A, no optimization refusals), approximately 1,374,960 B each. Restoring the old tonemapping Map iteration in update increased its bucket from 82,880 to 270,080 B/window (+187,200 B of ordinary iteration) but `bun test ./diagnostics/readback-allocation/render.node.ts` still PASSED. Restoring the fresh frame-encoder descriptor similarly increased frameEncoder from 81,600 to 100,800 B/window (+19,200 B) while the real gate PASSED. Both changes were reverted. Thus the required known-site red with a measured window is absent, not supplied by the synthetic test. Restoring the resolver arrow did fail the real gate, but only by refusing all three windows for '(anonymous)' optimization, with no byte reading or named allocation site. The exception is more permissive than 'later allocation on that line': these are function declaration lines, so allocations elsewhere in each function, and inlined callees, disappear too. 2. Existing allocations already excluded are not the promised single-use objects. A captured raw Inspector profile has performance.now nodes under recordMainPass with 5,760 B per 60-frame chunk (credited to phases.ts:300). Temporarily removing that function's six timing reads/subtractions lowered its aggregate from ~198,800 to 112,400 B/window, with all windows qualifying. The scheduler bucket includes Node setImmediate/Immediate/initAsyncResource/ref frames, not just command-buffer creation. Omitting world.gpu.sync experimentally lowered scheduler.step from 234,640 to 81,600 B/window. CONTRIBUTING requires a submission fence for runApp and staging, but the unit's explicit exception does not include these promise/reaction/timer allocations. They cannot silently become 'single-use GPU objects'. 3. Surface view is not single-use: the measured subject's CanvasContext returns a persistent texture. Temporarily caching its view reduced substrate.ts:280 from 175,360 to 93,760 B/window, without window refusals or validation errors. The texture view portion is avoidable for this subject and persistent attachTexture targets; the residual bucket also is not proved to be the view. A browser swapchain's lifetime is a separate issue, not evidence that this allocation is required here. Attribution notes: current AllocationSite collapses the profile to function/source line, but the underlying Inspector profile includes stacks, columns and native/builtin frames; it has no allocation-object type field. Keeping stacks distinguishes performance.now and Node timers, whereas sampled bytes directly on an optimized JS frame do not distinguish native binding work from our inlined JS. Native binding attribution needs controlled isolation, not blanket line filtering. Removing setBindGroup and dispatch in preprocess left 320 B/window at setBound (versus 640), while removing executeBundles eliminated renderColor's 320 B/window; the latter is bundle replay, not the claimed draw call. These are 32-byte-per-chunk effects, not proof of required single-use pass objects or browser allocation behavior. Frame/pass object creation at world:205, phases:300, cluster:1374/1480, preprocess:212 and tonemapping:133 is plausible, but exclusive attribution of all their bytes was not established; the counterexamples above disprove that general attribution rule. Independently examined the full candidate diff, unit Steady allocation and stage 9, AGENTS, CONTRIBUTING GPU tables/Readback, tigerstyle referent, sampler and raw profile, submission code/cardinality test, and all eight fix classes. No functional regression found in those fixes: resolver context is world-owned; descriptor/list and last encoder are bounded; indirect line record retains the four u32 fields; Map pruning and caster membership preserve semantics; shared matrices run after BeginFrame packing; Float64 position scratch preserves double-valued coordinates. Ran rendering/shadow/transparent/line/text and frame-encoder sets: 81 pass across 31 files. Active compute frames assert encoder/finish/submit cardinality; idle checks only submit/fence, since observeFrameSubmit is false there, so idle encoder/finish counts are not actually checked. Rendering-composition cardinality is not observed by this compute-only test. Remaining uncertainty: not every GPU bucket was separately no-op isolated, and browser binding allocation was not measured. This cannot support landing the narrowed allocation claim. All temporary tracked mutations were restored; worktree clean. Verdict: corrections.
